// New Zealand Area Codes, on the local calling areas of data.js. A landline number is
// the area code (03, 04, 06, 07, 09) plus seven digits; the NAD number register assigns the first three or four of them,
// block by block, to one of 102 local calling areas (LICAs). Three levels, coarse to fine: the 5 area codes, the 24 main
// calling areas (a main LICA with its minor ones, NAD "LICA Areas") and the 102 calling areas.
//
// Every block of the register counts, the newer ones of all carriers included. Because of them the digit after the area
// code alone places a number nowhere: "03 3" is mostly Christchurch, but has blocks in all 49 areas of 03. So a question
// draws one real block of the answer and shows as many of its digits as it takes to tell the answer among all blocks:
// "(03) 34" is enough for Christchurch, "(03) 206" for Edendale, a carrier's scattered block needs all four digits.
// Blocks are drawn by size (10,000 or 1,000 numbers) times the answer's numbers under the same first two digits, so an
// area's big ranges come up most and its scattered blocks now and then.

